            PQ("0.20 kg of water at 90 °C is added to 0.30 kg of water at 20 °C. Find the final temperature, assuming no heat loss.",
               r"$0.20(90 - \theta) = 0.30(\theta - 20)$, so $18 - 0.20\theta = 0.30\theta - 6$, so $24 = 0.50\theta$ and $\theta = 48\,{}^\circ$C."),
            PQ("Water is used as the coolant in a car radiator. Explain why it is a good choice.",
               "Water has a high specific heat capacity, so it absorbs a large amount of heat from the engine for a small rise in its own temperature."),
            TIP("Specific heat capacity appears in Paper 1 and Paper 2, in definitions, calculations and explanations. Quote the definition in full, write the formula, and give units (J/kg °C).", title="Exam link"),
            SUM(r"Specific heat capacity is the energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C. Unit: J/(kg °C).",
                r"$Q = mc\Delta\theta$, and for electrical heating $Q = Pt = VIt$.",
                "Heat capacity $C = mc$ is the energy needed to raise the temperature of the whole object by 1 °C.",
                "When objects mix with no heat loss, heat lost by the hotter one equals heat gained by the cooler one."),
