    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 14. Change of state and latent heat
    lesson(
        module=HEAT,
        topic="Change of state and latent heat",
        title="Change of state and specific latent heat",
        slug="phb-change-of-state-latent-heat",
        minutes=26,
        summary="During melting and boiling, energy is supplied but the temperature stays constant. Specific latent heat is the energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance, Q = mL.",
        objectives=[
            "Name the changes of state and describe them in terms of particles",
            "Interpret a heating curve and explain the flat parts",
            "Define specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization and use Q = mL",
            "Distinguish evaporation from boiling and explain cooling by evaporation",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Melting and boiling", "Heating curve", "Specific latent heat", "Q = mL", "Evaporation"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("A seller at the market puts ice blocks in a cooler box with bottles of drink. The ice melts slowly but the water stays cold. "
              "A kettle of water on a gas stove boils for a long time, and the thermometer stays at 100 °C even though the flame keeps burning."),
            T("Where does all the energy go? It is used to **change the state** of the substance, not to raise its temperature."),
            H("The three states and changes of state"),
            IMG("f5ph-b-states.svg",
                "Three boxes. Solid: particles packed closely in a regular pattern, vibrating about fixed positions. Liquid: particles close together but in an irregular arrangement, able to slide past each other. Gas: particles far apart, moving quickly in random directions.",
                "Figure 1. Particles in a solid, a liquid and a gas."),
            KEY("Changes of state", "",
                cards=[["Melting", "Solid to liquid, at the melting point."], ["Freezing", "Liquid to solid, at the same temperature."],
                       ["Boiling or evaporation", "Liquid to gas."], ["Condensation", "Gas to liquid."]]),
            D("**Specific latent heat** is the energy needed to change the state of **1 kg** of a substance **without changing its temperature**.", title="Latent heat",
              formula=r"Q = m L", note=r"$Q$ in joules, $m$ in kg, $L$ in J/kg."),
            KEY("Two latent heats of water", "Values used in this lesson (they will be given in exam questions):",
                cards=[["Specific latent heat of fusion of ice", r"$L_f = 3.4 \times 10^{5}$ J/kg. The energy to melt 1 kg of ice at 0 °C."],
                       ["Specific latent heat of vaporization of water", r"$L_v = 2.3 \times 10^{6}$ J/kg. The energy to turn 1 kg of water at 100 °C into steam."]],
                note="Textbooks use slightly different values (for example 3.3 × 10⁵ or 3.36 × 10⁵ for ice). Check your textbook and use the value in the question."),
            T("The energy does not raise the temperature. It is used to **break the bonds** (forces) between the particles. In melting, the particles are freed from fixed positions. In boiling, they are pulled far apart. "
              "It takes much more energy to boil than to melt, because the particles must be separated completely."),
            H("The heating curve"),
            IMG("f5ph-b-heating-curve.svg",
                "A graph of temperature against time for ice heated steadily. The temperature rises from minus 20 degrees to 0 degrees as ice warms, stays at 0 degrees while the ice melts, rises to 100 degrees as the water warms, stays at 100 degrees while the water boils, then rises as steam is heated.",
                "Figure 2. The heating curve of ice. The flat parts are the changes of state."),
            T("Where the line **slopes**, the energy raises the temperature (and the kinetic energy of the particles). Where it is **flat**, the energy is the latent heat changing the state. "
              "The boiling flat part is longer than the melting one, because more energy is needed to boil than to melt."),
            EX("Worked example 1: melting ice", intro=(
                r"How much energy is needed to melt 0.50 kg of ice at 0 °C? ($L_f = 3.4 \times 10^5$ J/kg)"),
               steps=[
                   S("Use Q = mL", math=r"Q = 0.50 \times 3.4 \times 10^{5}"),
                   S("Answer", math=r"Q = 1.7 \times 10^{5} \text{ J} = 170 \text{ kJ}"),
               ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: boiling water", intro=(
                r"A 2000 W electric kettle is switched on and keeps heating after the water has started boiling at 100 °C. How long does it take to boil away 0.10 kg of water? ($L_v = 2.3 \times 10^6$ J/kg)"),
               steps=[
                   S("Energy needed to vaporize 0.10 kg", math=r"Q = 0.10 \times 2.3 \times 10^{6} = 2.3 \times 10^{5} \text{ J}"),
                   S("Time = energy ÷ power", math=r"t = \frac{2.3 \times 10^{5}}{2000} = 115 \text{ s}"),
               ]),
            EX("Worked example 3: ice to warm water in stages", intro=(
                r"Find the energy needed to change 0.10 kg of ice at $-10$ °C into water at 20 °C. ($c_{\text{ice}} = 2100$, $c_{\text{water}} = 4200$ J/kg °C, $L_f = 3.4 \times 10^5$ J/kg)"),
               steps=[
                   S("Stage 1: warm the ice from −10 °C to 0 °C", math=r"Q_1 = 0.10 \times 2100 \times 10 = 2100 \text{ J}"),
                   S("Stage 2: melt the ice at 0 °C", math=r"Q_2 = 0.10 \times 3.4 \times 10^{5} = 34\,000 \text{ J}"),
                   S("Stage 3: warm the water from 0 °C to 20 °C", math=r"Q_3 = 0.10 \times 4200 \times 20 = 8400 \text{ J}"),
                   S("Add the stages", math=r"Q = 2100 + 34\,000 + 8400 = 44\,500 \text{ J}"),
               ]),
            TIP("In a multi-stage problem, work out each stage separately: **mcΔθ** where the temperature changes and **mL** where the state changes. Then add them."),
            H("Evaporation and boiling"),
            KEY("Evaporation and boiling compared", "",
                cards=[["Evaporation", "Happens at any temperature, only at the **surface**. The fastest (most energetic) particles escape, so the liquid left behind cools."],
                       ["Boiling", "Happens only at the boiling point, **throughout** the liquid, with bubbles of vapour forming inside."]]),
            T("Because evaporation takes away the highest-energy particles, it causes **cooling**. Sweat evaporating cools the skin. Water in a porous clay pot stays cooler than the air around it. "
              "Evaporation is faster with a higher temperature, a larger surface area, wind (moving air) and dry air."),
            T("**Steam** at 100 °C causes a worse burn than boiling water at 100 °C, because when the steam condenses on skin it releases its large latent heat as well."),
            QUIZ("Ice at 0 °C is heated steadily. While it is melting, what happens to the temperature of the ice-water mixture?",
                 ["It rises steadily", "It stays at 0 °C", "It falls below 0 °C", "It rises quickly and then falls"], 1,
                 "During a change of state the energy supplied is used to separate particles, not to raise the temperature. The temperature stays at 0 °C until all the ice has melted.",
                 hint="Look at the flat part of the heating curve.",
                 feedback=["The energy is used to melt the ice, not to raise the temperature.", "",
                           "Heating cannot make the mixture colder.", "The temperature does not fall. It stays constant, then rises when all the ice is water."]),
            MISTAKE("Using specific heat capacity during a change of state",
                    "During melting or boiling use $Q = mL$ (the temperature does not change). Use $Q = mc\\Delta\\theta$ only when the temperature changes without a change of state."),
            MISTAKE("Saying boiling and evaporation are the same",
                    "Evaporation occurs at any temperature at the surface. Boiling occurs at one temperature throughout the liquid."),
            PQ("Find the energy needed to melt 2.0 kg of ice at 0 °C. ($L_f = 3.4 \\times 10^5$ J/kg)",
               r"$Q = mL = 2.0 \times 3.4 \times 10^{5} = 6.8 \times 10^{5}$ J."),
            PQ("Find the energy needed to turn 0.25 kg of water at 100 °C into steam at 100 °C. ($L_v = 2.3 \\times 10^6$ J/kg)",
               r"$Q = 0.25 \times 2.3 \times 10^{6} = 5.75 \times 10^{5}$ J."),
            PQ("A heater of power 500 W is used to melt ice at 0 °C for 10 minutes. How much ice melts if all the heat is used? ($L_f = 3.4 \\times 10^5$ J/kg)",
               r"$Q = Pt = 500 \times 600 = 3.0 \times 10^{5}$ J. $m = Q/L = 3.0 \times 10^5 / 3.4 \times 10^5 \approx 0.88$ kg."),
            PQ("Explain why a person feels cold when wet skin is exposed to a breeze.",
               "Water evaporates from the skin. The fastest particles escape, taking energy with them, so the skin cools. The breeze carries away the vapour and increases the rate of evaporation."),
            TIP("Latent heat appears in Paper 1 (heating curves) and Paper 2 (calculations with mL and mcΔθ). Give the definition in words: energy to change state of 1 kg without a change in temperature.", title="Exam link"),
            SUM("Latent heat is the energy used to change state without changing temperature, so heating curves are flat during melting and boiling.",
                r"Specific latent heat is the energy needed to change the state of 1 kg: $Q = mL$ (J/kg).",
                "For stages with temperature changes, use $mc\\Delta\\theta$. For stages with state changes, use $mL$.",
                "Evaporation happens at the surface at any temperature and causes cooling. Boiling happens throughout the liquid at the boiling point."),
        ],
    ),
